INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S QUARTERS - NIGHT

A circular room with a cast-iron stove, six cots, shuttered windows. A storm throws rain against the glass. Candle stubs gutter on the sill.

ALDER, forties, a coast guard captain, stands near the stove. POSY, twenty, a young lighthouse apprentice, huddles on a cot. GARRICK, fifties, a retired seaman, sits cleaning a rifle. TILDA, thirties, a nurse, bandages a cut on RUDO's arm. RUDO, thirties, a fisherman, grimaces.

Something thumps on the roof.

ALDER
(to the room)
Nobody panic.

POSY
It's the wind.

GARRICK
That wasn't the wind.

TILDA
(whispering)
Hold still, Rudo.

RUDO
I'm holding still.

Another thump. Dust sifts from the ceiling.

ALDER
(muttering)
Come on, come on.

POSY
(to Alder)
Captain, what is it?

ALDER
I don't know.

The stove door rattles. The windows shudder. Then silence.

A knock at the door. Three slow knocks.

They all look at Alder. He steps forward, hand on his holster, and slides back the bolt.

On the threshold stands MR. PENHALLOW, sixties, drenched, in a sou'wester too small for his frame. He smiles with all his teeth.

MR. PENHALLOW
Good evening. I'm so sorry to disturb you. My boat has been lost.

ALDER
Come in out of the rain.

MR. PENHALLOW
You are kind.

He steps in. The candle flames bend toward him. Garrick lifts the rifle.

GARRICK
(low)
Captain.

ALDER
I see it.

POSY
(whispering)
His feet.

No one looks down. Nobody dares to. The puddle spreading from Penhallow's boots is dark, thick, and not rainwater.
